Usage: goodfetch-foxhound events [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  subscribe  Subscribes to the engine's events. The call stays in flight, sending its events in `$/events` batches, never more than the credits granted with `$/credit`, until it is cancelled. No producer waits for a subscription: a lossless event is never dropped unseen, and a subscription whose queue is full falls behind, dropping its queued lossless events, and next sends `events.resync`, then the stream. A telemetry event is coalesced with the unsent one of the same kind and scope, and counts it as `dropped`. Given `after`, the last event read, it resumes after it, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it or another engine instance gave it, as after a Daemon restart. Given `system_info`, it also takes the Resource governor's periodic `engine.system_info`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound events subscribe --help
Subscribes to the engine's events. The call stays in flight, sending its events in `$/events` batches, never more than the credits granted with `$/credit`, until it is cancelled. No producer waits for a subscription: a lossless event is never dropped unseen, and a subscription whose queue is full falls behind, dropping its queued lossless events, and next sends `events.resync`, then the stream. A telemetry event is coalesced with the unsent one of the same kind and scope, and counts it as `dropped`. Given `after`, the last event read, it resumes after it, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it or another engine instance gave it, as after a Daemon restart. Given `system_info`, it also takes the Resource governor's periodic `engine.system_info`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound events subscribe [OPTIONS] --follow

Options:
      --follow         Prints what the call sends as it arrives, until it ends
      --after <AFTER>  The last event the Client read, `{engine_instance, position}`: the subscription resumes after it, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it or another engine instance gave it, as after a Daemon restart. Absent, it starts with the next event
      --system-info    Whether it also takes `engine.system_info`, the Resource governor's periodic readings; false by default, so a subscription that reads only what changes sees no periodic event
      --json           Prints what the call sends as one JSON object per line
      --attached       Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon         Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound needs-you --help
Calls the catalog's `needs_you` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound needs-you [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list     Lists what waits for a person across every Run in the Run index and the stores beside it, the longest waiting first, a page at a time: `after` takes the `next` of the page before. `kinds` keeps the items of those kinds. Each change after the answer arrives as `needs_you.changed` on the event stream
  dismiss  Hides an item of "Needs you" whose source lets a person dismiss it, for every Client, and answers whether it did; an item the list no longer holds, or one only resolving it removes, stays as it is. The item leaves the list with `needs_you.changed`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound needs-you list --help
Lists what waits for a person across every Run in the Run index and the stores beside it, the longest waiting first, a page at a time: `after` takes the `next` of the page before. `kinds` keeps the items of those kinds. Each change after the answer arrives as `needs_you.changed` on the event stream

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound needs-you list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --after <AFTER>  The `next` of the page read before: the list goes on after it
      --kinds <KINDS>  Only the items of these kinds; every kind when absent
      --json           Prints the result as JSON
      --attached       Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon         Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound needs-you dismiss --help
Hides an item of "Needs you" whose source lets a person dismiss it, for every Client, and answers whether it did; an item the list no longer holds, or one only resolving it removes, stays as it is. The item leaves the list with `needs_you.changed`

