      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run --help
Calls the catalog's `run` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new-page          Opens a page in the Run's default Session, which the first call opens with the Run's settings and every later call shares; `page.session()` reaches it. The first page launches the Run's browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call
  new-session       Opens a Session of the Run's own: its own browser context, with its own cookies, storage, identity and proxy, sharing the Run's browser process, so two Sessions of a Run never share a login. It opens from `source`, or else from the Run's `session.source`, or fresh; `session.source` in its own `settings` is refused with `setting_not_applicable`. The Run's first Session launches its browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call
  start             Starts a Run its program drives, and answers once it is started. A Run ID names the Run for good, and a start under one acts on what the Run index holds (ADR 0102): no such Run begins it, `new`; an interrupted Run, stopped, failed, or running with no live writer, resumes from its committed state at the next run epoch, `resumed`, as `run.resume` does; a finished Run is answered as it ended with no handler called, `ended`. A Run live with a writer, in this engine or another, fails with `run_in_use`; a Run started with other inputs or by another caller with `run_conflict` listing each difference; a Run the Run index lists whose state is missing or unreadable with `run_state_unavailable`, never starting afresh
  finish            Lets a Run finish and answers its Run report: a Run its program drives ends at once, `finished` and `complete`; a crawl ends once its work items are done, and a keep-alive crawl (`crawl.keep_alive`), which waits for new work while nothing is left, ends once this call lets it and its work items are done. A Run something stopped first answers its report saying `stopped`. A Run that ended `failed` fails the call with the error that failed it, and a write at its end that failed, such as its Plan file's, with that write's error. Cancelling the call, or its `timeout` passing, which fails it with `deadline_exceeded`, ends only the wait
  stop              Stops a Run at its next safe point with a Checkpoint, `stopped` and `incomplete` as an abort by its program, resumable by its Run ID, and answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. The wait is bounded by `timeout.close`, or the call's `timeout`: past it, the call fails with `deadline_exceeded` and the Run still stops at its next safe point. A Run that ended `failed` fails the call as `run.finish` does
  abort             Stops a Run at its next safe point, `stopped` and `incomplete`: no attempt in flight commits, and its work items return to pending. Answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. A Run whose engine died is stopped by this engine, which takes its writer role at the next run epoch; a Run another engine writes is stopped by that engine, the owner the Run index names
  usage             Answers what a Run used, as the Usage ledger charged it: its model calls and tokens, browser time, proxy bytes, requests, paid solves and Paid fetches, each Limit that applies to it with what is left of it, and whether it is uncapped. An ended Run answers its report's Usage
  progress          Reads a Run's progress: what it does and why it waits. Without `after`, it answers at once; given `after`, the position of the value the reader last read, it waits for a change and answers the latest value, so values in between are coalesced. The value whose `last` is true ends the sequence: the Run ended, and its `lifecycle` and `stop_reason` say how, or no engine works it (`writer: none`) until a start with its Run ID resumes it. A Run another engine works is read through that engine, the owner the Run index names, and one no engine works from its committed state; a read never takes the Run's writer lock. A Run ID the Run index does not list fails with `run_not_found`
  report            Reads a Run's report: at once for a Run that ended, and at its end for a live one, which the read never ends. A Run another engine works is read through that engine, and one no engine works from its committed state, never taking its writer lock. A Run that has no live writer and has not ended fails with `run_interrupted`, whose `fix` names a start with its Run ID, and a Run ID the Run index does not list with `run_not_found`
  list              Lists the Runs of the Run index of the user and major, of either Product (ADRs 0031 and 0098): each row with its tags, Lifecycle, Verdict, Completeness, start and end, the engine that owns it while it is live, and its presence, a Run whose state is gone `missing` and one discarded on purpose `erased`, never left out. The engine filters by tags, the Recipe, Schedule, occurrence and trigger its reserved `goodfetch.` tags name, Product, each axis, start and end time, and a text found in its Run ID or tags, and sorts by the key given; Watch runs are left out unless `watch_runs` asks for them (ADR 0036)
  checkpoint        Writes a Checkpoint of a live Run now, through SQLite's online backup, where `location` says or where the engine keeps the Run's Checkpoints, and answers it once its file is complete, with its size, digest, run epoch and journal position. A Checkpoint the engine cannot write fails with `file_write_failed` and leaves no file
  pause             Pauses a live Run: checkpoints it, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `pause` and resumable by its Run ID with `run.resume`, after a restart too. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint
  migrate           Migrates a live Run: checkpoints it where `location` says or where the engine keeps its Checkpoints, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `migrate`, to be resumed from that Checkpoint on another machine. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint
  resume            Resumes an interrupted Run from its committed state, its run file on this machine or the Checkpoint `checkpoint` names: it takes the Run's writer lock and the next run epoch, so every write of an earlier writer is refused, and redoes the work since that state; its recorded Clean-up rules digest is kept. A Run this engine runs already fails with `run_in_use`, as one another engine writes does; a finished one is answered as it ended, `ended`. A Run bound to another machine fails with `session_bound_to_machine`, and a Checkpoint of a format version this engine cannot read with `format_version_unsupported`
  seeds             Lists each seed's answer, in the order the crawl's start gave them, as the Frontier answered it when the Run began: added, with its crawl request's ID and its URL as parsed, or not added, with its reason (`duplicate`, `domain_policy`, `unsupported_scheme`, `invalid_url` or a cap's name). A relative seed is `invalid_url`, since nothing gives it a base. So a crawl that does nothing says why. Read from the Run's committed state, so it works on a Run no program holds
  add-seed          Adds one seed to a running crawl, `run.addSeed`, committed at once, and answers as an enqueue does: added, with its crawl request's ID and its URL as parsed, or not added, with its reason (`duplicate`, `unsupported_scheme`, `invalid_url` or a cap's name). A relative seed is `invalid_url`. An idle `crawl.keep_alive` crawl takes it and goes on. Through a handler's context, naming its `attempt`, it is bound to that attempt: once the attempt ended it fails with `attempt_ended`. A Run this engine does not run as a live crawl, or a crawl already ending its Run, fails with `run_not_found`
  add-seeds         Takes a stream of seeds for a running crawl, `run.addSeeds`, and answers each as `run.add_seed` does, in order, so a million seeds never sit in memory. The engine grants the call 256 credits with `$/credit`, one seed each; the Client sends seeds in `$/seeds {id, seeds, end?}` batches, never more than its credits, and the engine commits each batch, sends its answers in one `$/enqueued {id, answers}` and grants as many credits again. Once it answered the batch marked `end`, the call answers how many seeds were added and how many were not. Cancelling it keeps the seeds already answered. A batch beyond the credits granted, or a seed that is no seed, fails the call with `invalid_params`; a Run this engine does not run as a live crawl, or a crawl already ending its Run, fails with `run_not_found`
  records           Reads the committed Records of one Dataset of a crawl's Run in number order, from the first, or after the number `after`, which resumes a reader from the last Record it handled. Each Record goes in a `$/record` notification with its number, its value, its request's ID and URL and when it was committed, one per credit granted with `$/credit`. While the Run is live it follows the Run, sending each Record once it commits; once the Run has ended, or has no live writer, and every Record is sent, it answers how many it sent and the last one's number. With `through`, such as the number `dataset.page` answers as the last committed, it reads no Record numbered past it and answers once it sent every Record through it, a live Run's included. Records are read from the Run's committed state on a connection of their own, so a reader that grants no credit never slows the crawl. With `wall` set to `true` it reads only the Records whose page was read past a Wall, which their Provenance marks, and with `false` only the others (ADR 0052). A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`
  export            Writes an Export of one Dataset of a crawl's Run: the Records it committed when the call is made, in number order, in the format named: `json`, one array of the Records' values, indented as `export.json.indent` says; `jsonl`, one value per line; `csv`, `xlsx`, `html_table` and `markdown`, a table of one column per field seen in any of its Records, in first-seen order, and one row per Record; `parquet`, `avro` and `arrow_ipc`, the same columns typed: a column whose values are all `true` or `false` is boolean, all whole numbers a 64-bit integer, all numbers a 64-bit float, and any other a string holding each cell's text, every column nullable, an Avro field's name made a valid Avro name with the field's own in its `doc`; `xml`, a root element named by `export.xml.root` holding one element per Record named by `export.xml.record`, each field an element of its name (`field` with a `name` attribute where its name is no XML name), an object's fields as elements, an array's values as `item` elements, and `null` an empty element with `xsi:nil="true"`; `rss` and `atom`, a feed named after the Dataset of one item or entry per Record, its title, link, date and summary taken from the fields the `export.feed.*` settings name. Every text Export is UTF-8 with no BOM unless `export.csv.bom` puts one on CSV, characters written as themselves (a character XML 1.0 cannot hold as U+FFFD in XML and feeds) and each value as it was committed: a table cell holds a string as its text, a number, `true` or `false` as JSON writes it, nothing for `null` or a field its Record lacks, and an object or array as its JSON text, or, in CSV under `export.csv.nested: flatten`, an object's fields in dotted columns. A Record whose page was read past a Wall carries its Wall marker, `{kind, vendor?}` as its Provenance holds it, as a `#wall` field in every format but the feeds, where it is a category of the scheme `urn:goodfetch:wall` naming its kind; `wall` set to `true` writes only those Records, `false` only the others. `compression` `gzip` or `zstd` compresses the whole file. Its `settings` and `project` are the run layer and the project configuration its `export.*` settings resolve under; a value the format cannot take fails with `setting_invalid`. Answers the Export, with its Record count, its last Record's number, its size and its SHA-256, only once its file is whole in the Run's folder, where `export.save_as` and `export.stream` read it. Records committed after the call starts are not in it. A write the engine's storage refuses fails with `file_write_failed`, side `engine`, leaving no unfinished file, as does a call while that storage is at the `critical` level, before any byte is written. A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`
  outcomes          Reads a crawl's outcomes from its committed state, in commit order: from the first, after a position, or with `after: "now"` from the next commit. Its filters, `kinds`, `labels` and `codes` (an Error code any of the request's failed attempts failed with), run in the engine. While the Run is live and nothing new matches, it waits for the next commit; a page says `ended` once the Run has ended, or has no live writer, and nothing more matches. A reader never slows the Run. A request `run.retry_failed` re-admits ends again, and its later outcome supersedes the earlier one by `request.id`
  failed-requests   Reads from a crawl's committed state the requests whose latest outcome is `failed`, and with `blocked` also `blocked`, each with that outcome: exactly those `run.retry_failed` re-admits with the same option. A skip is a decision and is never listed
  retry-failed      Re-admits, in the same Run and its Datasets, every request of a crawl whose latest outcome is `failed`, and with `blocked` also `blocked`: exactly those `run.failed_requests` lists with the same option. Each keeps its ID, Label, Payload and depth; its attempts count on, with a fresh `crawl.retry.max`. A skip is a decision and is never re-admitted. It answers how many it re-admitted, 0 when there was nothing to retry. On a crawl this engine runs, the requests join its work at once, unless the crawl is already ending its Run: the call then waits for that end and re-admits them as on an ended Run. On an ended Run with requests to retry, the Run returns to `running` under `settings`, as a resume does, its Verdict and Completeness given anew when it ends, and the calling Connection serves its handlers. A Run with no writer that has not ended fails with `run_interrupted`: resume it first. It is not sent again after a lost Connection, which fails it with `connection_lost`: `run.failed_requests` then lists the requests still to retry
  rejected-records  Reads one Dataset's rejection table from a crawl's committed state: each Record rejected rather than committed, numbered from 1 in commit order, with the Record as JSON, the reason, each issue's path, the Hook when one rejected it, and the request's ID and URL. It starts at the first entry, or after `after`, the number a reader last handled. While the Run is live and nothing follows, it waits for the next commit; a page says `ended` once the Run has ended, or has no live writer, and no entry follows. A reader never slows the Run. A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run new-page --help
Opens a page in the Run's default Session, which the first call opens with the Run's settings and every later call shares; `page.session()` reaches it. The first page launches the Run's browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run new-page [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run

Options:
      --set <KEY=VALUE>    Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->  Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run new-session --help
Opens a Session of the Run's own: its own browser context, with its own cookies, storage, identity and proxy, sharing the Run's browser process, so two Sessions of a Run never share a login. It opens from `source`, or else from the Run's `session.source`, or fresh; `session.source` in its own `settings` is refused with `setting_not_applicable`. The Run's first Session launches its browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run new-session [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run

Options:
      --set <KEY=VALUE>    Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->  Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --source <SOURCE>    `session.source` for this Session alone: left out, the Run's or the connection's applies; `null`, the Session opens fresh (ADRs 0083 and 0092)
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
