      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run start --help
Starts a Run its program drives, and answers once it is started. A Run ID names the Run for good, and a start under one acts on what the Run index holds (ADR 0102): no such Run begins it, `new`; an interrupted Run, stopped, failed, or running with no live writer, resumes from its committed state at the next run epoch, `resumed`, as `run.resume` does; a finished Run is answered as it ended with no handler called, `ended`. A Run live with a writer, in this engine or another, fails with `run_in_use`; a Run started with other inputs or by another caller with `run_conflict` listing each difference; a Run the Run index lists whose state is missing or unreadable with `run_state_unavailable`, never starting afresh

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run start [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --limit <KIND=AMOUNT>  Holds the Run to AMOUNT of the Usage kind KIND: a count, such as `requests=500`, or a price with its currency, such as `paid_fetch=0.50USD`; repeatable, and a start that names the Run again only lowers a Limit
      --project <PROJECT>    The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083)
      --run-id <RUN_ID>      The Run's ID; the engine generates a lowercase ULID when it is absent. An existing ID reaches that Run when the caller and the inputs match, as `run.start` says, and fails with `run_conflict` otherwise
      --set <KEY=VALUE>      Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->    Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --tags <TAGS>          The Run's opaque tags
      --color <WHEN>         When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --json                 Prints the result as JSON
      --attached             Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon               Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help                 Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run finish --help
Lets a Run finish and answers its Run report: a Run its program drives ends at once, `finished` and `complete`; a crawl ends once its work items are done, and a keep-alive crawl (`crawl.keep_alive`), which waits for new work while nothing is left, ends once this call lets it and its work items are done. A Run something stopped first answers its report saying `stopped`. A Run that ended `failed` fails the call with the error that failed it, and a write at its end that failed, such as its Plan file's, with that write's error. Cancelling the call, or its `timeout` passing, which fails it with `deadline_exceeded`, ends only the wait

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run finish [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --timeout <DURATION>  How long the call waits for the Run's end, in milliseconds, at least 1, as `timeout.run` for this call alone: absent or `null` for none, since the Run's own `timeout.run` bounds it (ADR 0080). Past it, the call fails with `deadline_exceeded` and only the wait ends
      --json                Prints the result as JSON
      --attached            Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon              Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --require-verified    Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --color <WHEN>        When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run stop --help
Stops a Run at its next safe point with a Checkpoint, `stopped` and `incomplete` as an abort by its program, resumable by its Run ID, and answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. The wait is bounded by `timeout.close`, or the call's `timeout`: past it, the call fails with `deadline_exceeded` and the Run still stops at its next safe point. A Run that ended `failed` fails the call as `run.finish` does

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run stop [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --timeout <DURATION>  How long the call waits for the Run's safe point, in milliseconds, at least 1: absent for the Run's `timeout.close`, `null` for none (ADR 0080). Past it, the call fails with `deadline_exceeded` and the Run still stops at its next safe point
      --json                Prints the result as JSON
      --attached            Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon              Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --require-verified    Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --color <WHEN>        When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run abort --help
Stops a Run at its next safe point, `stopped` and `incomplete`: no attempt in flight commits, and its work items return to pending. Answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. A Run whose engine died is stopped by this engine, which takes its writer role at the next run epoch; a Run another engine writes is stopped by that engine, the owner the Run index names

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run abort [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --forwarded         Set by an engine that forwards the abort to the owner the Run index names (ADR 0031): an engine that does not own the Run then answers `run_not_owned` instead of taking its writer role
      --json              Prints the result as JSON
      --attached          Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon            Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --require-verified  Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --color <WHEN>      When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help              Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run usage --help
Answers what a Run used, as the Usage ledger charged it: its model calls and tokens, browser time, proxy bytes, requests, paid solves and Paid fetches, each Limit that applies to it with what is left of it, and whether it is uncapped. An ended Run answers its report's Usage

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run usage [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run progress --help
Reads a Run's progress: what it does and why it waits. Without `after`, it answers at once; given `after`, the position of the value the reader last read, it waits for a change and answers the latest value, so values in between are coalesced. The value whose `last` is true ends the sequence: the Run ended, and its `lifecycle` and `stop_reason` say how, or no engine works it (`writer: none`) until a start with its Run ID resumes it. A Run another engine works is read through that engine, the owner the Run index names, and one no engine works from its committed state; a read never takes the Run's writer lock. A Run ID the Run index does not list fails with `run_not_found`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run progress [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --after <AFTER>  The position of the value the reader last read: the answer is the latest value after it, once one comes. Absent, the Run's progress at once
      --json           Prints the result as JSON
      --attached       Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon         Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run report --help
Reads a Run's report: at once for a Run that ended, and at its end for a live one, which the read never ends. A Run another engine works is read through that engine, and one no engine works from its committed state, never taking its writer lock. A Run that has no live writer and has not ended fails with `run_interrupted`, whose `fix` names a start with its Run ID, and a Run ID the Run index does not list with `run_not_found`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run report [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --json              Prints the result as JSON
      --attached          Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon            Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --require-verified  Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --color <WHEN>      When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help              Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run list --help
Lists the Runs of the Run index of the user and major, of either Product (ADRs 0031 and 0098): each row with its tags, Lifecycle, Verdict, Completeness, start and end, the engine that owns it while it is live, and its presence, a Run whose state is gone `missing` and one discarded on purpose `erased`, never left out. The engine filters by tags, the Recipe, Schedule, occurrence and trigger its reserved `goodfetch.` tags name, Product, each axis, start and end time, and a text found in its Run ID or tags, and sorts by the key given; Watch runs are left out unless `watch_runs` asks for them (ADR 0036)

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --completeness <COMPLETENESS>  Their Completeness: only Runs that ended have one
      --ended <ENDED>                When they ended: only Runs that ended match
      --lifecycle <LIFECYCLE>        Their Lifecycle
      --occurrence <OCCURRENCE>      The occurrence that started the Runs, by its ID
      --order <ORDER>                Which way, descending by default
      --product <PRODUCT>            The Product the Runs need (ADR 0098)
      --color <WHEN>                 When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --recipe <RECIPE>              The Recipe the Runs ran, by its name, as their reserved `goodfetch.recipe` tag names it
      --schedule <SCHEDULE>          The Schedule that started the Runs, by its ID
      --sort <SORT>                  What the rows are sorted by, when they started by default; rows that compare equal follow in Run ID order
      --started <STARTED>            When they started
      --tags <TAGS>                  Tags each row carries, every one of them
      --text <TEXT>                  Text found, ignoring case, in their Run ID or one of their tags, their Recipe's among them
      --trigger <TRIGGER>            What fired the occurrence that started the Runs
      --verdict <VERDICT>            Their Verdict: only Runs that ended have one
      --watch-runs                   Whether Watch runs are listed too; they are left out by default (ADR 0036)
      --json                         Prints the result as JSON
      --attached                     Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon                       Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help                         Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run checkpoint --help
Writes a Checkpoint of a live Run now, through SQLite's online backup, where `location` says or where the engine keeps the Run's Checkpoints, and answers it once its file is complete, with its size, digest, run epoch and journal position. A Checkpoint the engine cannot write fails with `file_write_failed` and leaves no file

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run checkpoint [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --location <LOCATION>  Where to write the Checkpoint, a path on the engine's machine; by default where the engine keeps the Run's Checkpoints, its own folder
      --json                 Prints the result as JSON
      --attached             Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon               Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>         When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                 Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run pause --help
Pauses a live Run: checkpoints it, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `pause` and resumable by its Run ID with `run.resume`, after a restart too. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run pause [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --json              Prints the result as JSON
      --attached          Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon            Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --require-verified  Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --color <WHEN>      When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help              Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run migrate --help
Migrates a live Run: checkpoints it where `location` says or where the engine keeps its Checkpoints, then stops it at its next safe point, `stopped` with the stop reason `migrate`, to be resumed from that Checkpoint on another machine. Answers its Run report, which names the Checkpoint

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run migrate [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --location <LOCATION>  Where to write the Checkpoint, a path on the engine's machine; by default where the engine keeps the Run's Checkpoints, its own folder
      --json                 Prints the result as JSON
      --attached             Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon               Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --require-verified     Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --color <WHEN>         When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                 Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run resume --help
Resumes an interrupted Run from its committed state, its run file on this machine or the Checkpoint `checkpoint` names: it takes the Run's writer lock and the next run epoch, so every write of an earlier writer is refused, and redoes the work since that state; its recorded Clean-up rules digest is kept. A Run this engine runs already fails with `run_in_use`, as one another engine writes does; a finished one is answered as it ended, `ended`. A Run bound to another machine fails with `session_bound_to_machine`, and a Checkpoint of a format version this engine cannot read with `format_version_unsupported`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run resume [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --checkpoint <CHECKPOINT>  A Checkpoint to resume from, a path on the engine's machine, which becomes the Run's state in this engine's run folder; by default the Run resumes from its run file on this machine
      --project <PROJECT>        The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083)
      --set <KEY=VALUE>          Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->        Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --json                     Prints the result as JSON
      --attached                 Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --color <WHEN>             When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --daemon                   Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help                     Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run seeds --help
Lists each seed's answer, in the order the crawl's start gave them, as the Frontier answered it when the Run began: added, with its crawl request's ID and its URL as parsed, or not added, with its reason (`duplicate`, `domain_policy`, `unsupported_scheme`, `invalid_url` or a cap's name). A relative seed is `invalid_url`, since nothing gives it a base. So a crawl that does nothing says why. Read from the Run's committed state, so it works on a Run no program holds

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run seeds [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run add-seed --help
Adds one seed to a running crawl, `run.addSeed`, committed at once, and answers as an enqueue does: added, with its crawl request's ID and its URL as parsed, or not added, with its reason (`duplicate`, `unsupported_scheme`, `invalid_url` or a cap's name). A relative seed is `invalid_url`. An idle `crawl.keep_alive` crawl takes it and goes on. Through a handler's context, naming its `attempt`, it is bound to that attempt: once the attempt ended it fails with `attempt_ended`. A Run this engine does not run as a live crawl, or a crawl already ending its Run, fails with `run_not_found`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run add-seed [OPTIONS] --seed <SEED> <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --attempt <ATTEMPT>  The attempt that adds it, when a handler adds it through its context's `run`; absent, the program adds it from outside any attempt
      --seed <SEED>        The seed
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run add-seeds --help
Takes a stream of seeds for a running crawl, `run.addSeeds`, and answers each as `run.add_seed` does, in order, so a million seeds never sit in memory. The engine grants the call 256 credits with `$/credit`, one seed each; the Client sends seeds in `$/seeds {id, seeds, end?}` batches, never more than its credits, and the engine commits each batch, sends its answers in one `$/enqueued {id, answers}` and grants as many credits again. Once it answered the batch marked `end`, the call answers how many seeds were added and how many were not. Cancelling it keeps the seeds already answered. A batch beyond the credits granted, or a seed that is no seed, fails the call with `invalid_params`; a Run this engine does not run as a live crawl, or a crawl already ending its Run, fails with `run_not_found`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run add-seeds [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --json          Prints what the call answers each batch of stdin's lines with as one JSON object per line
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run records --help
Reads the committed Records of one Dataset of a crawl's Run in number order, from the first, or after the number `after`, which resumes a reader from the last Record it handled. Each Record goes in a `$/record` notification with its number, its value, its request's ID and URL and when it was committed, one per credit granted with `$/credit`. While the Run is live it follows the Run, sending each Record once it commits; once the Run has ended, or has no live writer, and every Record is sent, it answers how many it sent and the last one's number. With `through`, such as the number `dataset.page` answers as the last committed, it reads no Record numbered past it and answers once it sent every Record through it, a live Run's included. Records are read from the Run's committed state on a connection of their own, so a reader that grants no credit never slows the crawl. With `wall` set to `true` it reads only the Records whose page was read past a Wall, which their Provenance marks, and with `false` only the others (ADR 0052). A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run records [OPTIONS] --dataset <DATASET> <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --follow             Prints what the call sends as it arrives, until it ends, rather than once it ended
      --after <AFTER>      The number of the last Record the reader handled: the call starts after it. Absent, it starts at the Dataset's first Record
      --dataset <DATASET>  The Dataset's name
      --through <THROUGH>  The number of the last Record to read, such as the last one committed when the reader began (`dataset.page`'s `through`): the call answers once it sent every Record numbered through it, waiting on a live Run for those not committed yet. Absent, it follows a live Run until the Run ends
      --wall <true|false>  `true` reads only the Records whose page was read past a Wall, `false` only the others; absent, every Record (ADR 0052) [possible values: true, false]
      --json               Prints what the call sent as one JSON array, or under `--follow` one JSON object per line
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run export --help
Writes an Export of one Dataset of a crawl's Run: the Records it committed when the call is made, in number order, in the format named: `json`, one array of the Records' values, indented as `export.json.indent` says; `jsonl`, one value per line; `csv`, `xlsx`, `html_table` and `markdown`, a table of one column per field seen in any of its Records, in first-seen order, and one row per Record; `parquet`, `avro` and `arrow_ipc`, the same columns typed: a column whose values are all `true` or `false` is boolean, all whole numbers a 64-bit integer, all numbers a 64-bit float, and any other a string holding each cell's text, every column nullable, an Avro field's name made a valid Avro name with the field's own in its `doc`; `xml`, a root element named by `export.xml.root` holding one element per Record named by `export.xml.record`, each field an element of its name (`field` with a `name` attribute where its name is no XML name), an object's fields as elements, an array's values as `item` elements, and `null` an empty element with `xsi:nil="true"`; `rss` and `atom`, a feed named after the Dataset of one item or entry per Record, its title, link, date and summary taken from the fields the `export.feed.*` settings name. Every text Export is UTF-8 with no BOM unless `export.csv.bom` puts one on CSV, characters written as themselves (a character XML 1.0 cannot hold as U+FFFD in XML and feeds) and each value as it was committed: a table cell holds a string as its text, a number, `true` or `false` as JSON writes it, nothing for `null` or a field its Record lacks, and an object or array as its JSON text, or, in CSV under `export.csv.nested: flatten`, an object's fields in dotted columns. A Record whose page was read past a Wall carries its Wall marker, `{kind, vendor?}` as its Provenance holds it, as a `#wall` field in every format but the feeds, where it is a category of the scheme `urn:goodfetch:wall` naming its kind; `wall` set to `true` writes only those Records, `false` only the others. `compression` `gzip` or `zstd` compresses the whole file. Its `settings` and `project` are the run layer and the project configuration its `export.*` settings resolve under; a value the format cannot take fails with `setting_invalid`. Answers the Export, with its Record count, its last Record's number, its size and its SHA-256, only once its file is whole in the Run's folder, where `export.save_as` and `export.stream` read it. Records committed after the call starts are not in it. A write the engine's storage refuses fails with `file_write_failed`, side `engine`, leaving no unfinished file, as does a call while that storage is at the `critical` level, before any byte is written. A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run export [OPTIONS] --format <FORMAT> --out <PATH|-> <RUN_ID> <DATASET>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>   The crawl's Run
  <DATASET>  The Dataset's name

Options:
      --compression <COMPRESSION>  The compression; absent, `none`
      --format <FORMAT>            The format
      --project <PROJECT>          The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083)
      --set <KEY=VALUE>            Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->          Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --wall <true|false>          `true` writes only the Records whose page was read past a Wall, `false` only the others; absent, every Record (ADR 0052) [possible values: true, false]
      --color <WHEN>               When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --out <PATH|->               Writes the Export's file to PATH, whole or not at all, or its bytes to standard output for `-`
      --json                       Prints the result as JSON
      --attached                   Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon                     Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help                       Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run outcomes --help
Reads a crawl's outcomes from its committed state, in commit order: from the first, after a position, or with `after: "now"` from the next commit. Its filters, `kinds`, `labels` and `codes` (an Error code any of the request's failed attempts failed with), run in the engine. While the Run is live and nothing new matches, it waits for the next commit; a page says `ended` once the Run has ended, or has no live writer, and nothing more matches. A reader never slows the Run. A request `run.retry_failed` re-admits ends again, and its later outcome supersedes the earlier one by `request.id`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run outcomes [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --follow           Asks for page after page, printing each item as it arrives, until the Run ends
      --after <AFTER>    Where to start: after this position, or `now` for the next commit on; absent, from the first outcome
      --codes <CODES>    Only outcomes of requests one of whose failed attempts failed with one of these Error codes; absent, every outcome
      --kinds <KINDS>    Only outcomes of these kinds; absent, every kind
      --labels <LABELS>  Only outcomes of requests of these Labels; absent, every Label
      --json             Prints the result as JSON, or under `--follow` each item as one JSON object per line
      --attached         Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --color <WHEN>     When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --daemon           Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help             Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run failed-requests --help
Reads from a crawl's committed state the requests whose latest outcome is `failed`, and with `blocked` also `blocked`, each with that outcome: exactly those `run.retry_failed` re-admits with the same option. A skip is a decision and is never listed

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run failed-requests [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --blocked       Also the requests whose latest outcome is `blocked`
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run retry-failed --help
Re-admits, in the same Run and its Datasets, every request of a crawl whose latest outcome is `failed`, and with `blocked` also `blocked`: exactly those `run.failed_requests` lists with the same option. Each keeps its ID, Label, Payload and depth; its attempts count on, with a fresh `crawl.retry.max`. A skip is a decision and is never re-admitted. It answers how many it re-admitted, 0 when there was nothing to retry. On a crawl this engine runs, the requests join its work at once, unless the crawl is already ending its Run: the call then waits for that end and re-admits them as on an ended Run. On an ended Run with requests to retry, the Run returns to `running` under `settings`, as a resume does, its Verdict and Completeness given anew when it ends, and the calling Connection serves its handlers. A Run with no writer that has not ended fails with `run_interrupted`: resume it first. It is not sent again after a lost Connection, which fails it with `connection_lost`: `run.failed_requests` then lists the requests still to retry

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run retry-failed [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --blocked            Also the requests whose latest outcome is `blocked`
      --project <PROJECT>  The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083), read as `settings` is
      --set <KEY=VALUE>    Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->  Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound run rejected-records --help
Reads one Dataset's rejection table from a crawl's committed state: each Record rejected rather than committed, numbered from 1 in commit order, with the Record as JSON, the reason, each issue's path, the Hook when one rejected it, and the request's ID and URL. It starts at the first entry, or after `after`, the number a reader last handled. While the Run is live and nothing follows, it waits for the next commit; a page says `ended` once the Run has ended, or has no live writer, and no entry follows. A reader never slows the Run. A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound run rejected-records [OPTIONS] --dataset <DATASET> <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run

Options:
      --follow             Asks for page after page, printing each item as it arrives, until the Run ends
      --after <AFTER>      The number of the last entry a reader handled; absent, from the first
      --dataset <DATASET>  The Dataset whose rejection table to read
      --json               Prints the result as JSON, or under `--follow` each item as one JSON object per line
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound session --help
Calls the catalog's `session` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound session [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new-page  Opens a page in the Session, with its cookies and storage, and its own settings. Once the Session is closed, it raises `closed`
  close     Closes the Session: its pages and its browser context close, and its pages' admissions go back to the Resource governor; a Borrowed browser is never closed. A later call on the Session or one of its pages raises `closed`, and closing it again changes nothing
  pages     Lists the Session's open pages, in the order they opened: those the program opened and the popups its pages opened. Once the Session is closed, it raises `closed`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound session new-page --help
Opens a page in the Session, with its cookies and storage, and its own settings. Once the Session is closed, it raises `closed`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound session new-page [OPTIONS] <SESSION_ID>

Arguments:
  <SESSION_ID>  The Session

Options:
      --set <KEY=VALUE>    Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->  Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound session close --help
Closes the Session: its pages and its browser context close, and its pages' admissions go back to the Resource governor; a Borrowed browser is never closed. A later call on the Session or one of its pages raises `closed`, and closing it again changes nothing

