$ goodfetch-foxhound page --help
Calls the catalog's `page` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound page [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  goto        Navigates the page to a URL and waits for its document's `load` event, or the state `wait_until` names, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A file the browser downloads, such as a PDF, answers no response and emits `page.download`. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`, a redirect loop `too_many_redirects`, a document whose body broke off `response_truncated`, unless the navigation answered at `commit`, and one past its `timeout`, or `timeout.navigation` without one, `deadline_exceeded`
  reload      Loads the page's document again and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`
  go-back     Goes back one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go back to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)
  go-forward  Goes forward one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go forward to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)
  formats     Reads the formats a request names from the page's current document, each read from one serialization of it, and answers only those, with the document's URL, its base URL and, when a format it names is read from the main content (`markdown` of the main content or fit, `html` of the main content, `text`), whether the document has main content. A document with no main content answers those formats empty and `main_content: "none"`, a value. It needs neither layout nor pixels, so every tier serves it
  read        Reads the named fields from the page's current document in one call and answers each one's value: a value field's from the first element its CSS or XPath locator finds, its text or one attribute, optionally a `match` group, then its Value transform; a list's from every element its locator finds; rows' from each element a base locator finds, each row one object of its fields read under that element. A value the page does not give is null, and `unread` names it with why: a row lacking a field keeps its other fields. It never waits: an absent element is an answer. It needs neither layout nor pixels, so every tier serves it, and in the browser it is one read of the page whatever the number of fields
  body        Reads a page body by its handle, for a Client that cannot read the handle's file: sends the body in `$/chunk` notifications of at most 64 KiB, one per credit granted with `$/credit`, then answers its size
  close       Closes the page, a popup as any other: its calls in flight raise `call_cancelled` naming the program, it emits `close`, and a later call on it raises `closed`, while closing it again changes nothing. Its `beforeunload` handlers do not run unless `run_before_unload` is set: the page then closes once they let it, a dialog they raise going to its dialog handlers, and the call answers without waiting for that

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

