$ goodfetch-foxhound response --help
Calls the catalog's `response` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound response [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  body  Reads the body of the response a page's request got, once the request finished, as a page body's handle: kept already for a response `page.wait_for_request_finished` answered, and otherwise read from the browser, on the session of the frame that loaded it. A body the browser no longer holds, or a redirect's, which has none, raises `body_unavailable`, whose fix names `page.wait_for_request_finished`; one that broke off raises `response_truncated`, never returned as whole. Past its `timeout`, `timeout.wait` when the call gives none, a request that has not ended fails it with `deadline_exceeded`; a page that ends under it raises what ended it, as a call on the page does

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound response body --help
Reads the body of the response a page's request got, once the request finished, as a page body's handle: kept already for a response `page.wait_for_request_finished` answered, and otherwise read from the browser, on the session of the frame that loaded it. A body the browser no longer holds, or a redirect's, which has none, raises `body_unavailable`, whose fix names `page.wait_for_request_finished`; one that broke off raises `response_truncated`, never returned as whole. Past its `timeout`, `timeout.wait` when the call gives none, a request that has not ended fails it with `deadline_exceeded`; a page that ends under it raises what ended it, as a call on the page does

