$ goodfetch-foxhound signing-key --help
Calls the catalog's `signing_key` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound signing-key [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  create     Makes a new Ed25519 key for an `identified` Session's Web Bot Auth signatures, from the OS generator, and keeps it as a private JSON Web Key in the item a `keychain:` or `secretfile:` Secret reference names; answers the reference, the key's ID and its public key, never the private key. An item that holds an Ed25519 key already keeps it and answers it with `created` false; one that holds anything else is refused. Set `identified.signing_key` to the reference, then export the key directory with `signing_key.directory`
  directory  Exports the Web Bot Auth key directory of the keys the Secret references name, for the user to serve at `/.well-known/http-message-signatures-directory` of the origin `identified.signature_agent` names: the body, a JSON key set of each key's public JSON Web Key named by its RFC 7638 thumbprint, and the `Content-Type`, `Signature-Input` and `Signature` fields its response carries, where each key signs the origin's authority with the tag `http-message-signatures-directory` to prove it holds the key. Valid for `valid_for` milliseconds, seven days by default; export it again before `expires`. A reference that gives no Ed25519 private JSON Web Key is refused

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound signing-key create --help
Makes a new Ed25519 key for an `identified` Session's Web Bot Auth signatures, from the OS generator, and keeps it as a private JSON Web Key in the item a `keychain:` or `secretfile:` Secret reference names; answers the reference, the key's ID and its public key, never the private key. An item that holds an Ed25519 key already keeps it and answers it with `created` false; one that holds anything else is refused. Set `identified.signing_key` to the reference, then export the key directory with `signing_key.directory`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound signing-key create [OPTIONS] --signing-key <SIGNING_KEY>

Options:
      --signing-key <SIGNING_KEY>  The Secret reference of the item the key is kept in, a `keychain:` or a `secretfile:` one, such as `keychain:goodfetch/signing-key`; the value `identified.signing_key` then takes
      --json                       Prints the result as JSON
      --attached                   Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon                     Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>               When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                       Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound signing-key directory --help
Exports the Web Bot Auth key directory of the keys the Secret references name, for the user to serve at `/.well-known/http-message-signatures-directory` of the origin `identified.signature_agent` names: the body, a JSON key set of each key's public JSON Web Key named by its RFC 7638 thumbprint, and the `Content-Type`, `Signature-Input` and `Signature` fields its response carries, where each key signs the origin's authority with the tag `http-message-signatures-directory` to prove it holds the key. Valid for `valid_for` milliseconds, seven days by default; export it again before `expires`. A reference that gives no Ed25519 private JSON Web Key is refused

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound signing-key directory [OPTIONS] --authority <AUTHORITY> --signing-keys <SIGNING_KEYS>

Options:
      --authority <AUTHORITY>        The authority of the origin that serves the directory, its host and its port when not the scheme's own, such as `bot.example`, which the signatures cover: the origin `identified.signature_agent` names
      --signing-keys <SIGNING_KEYS>  The Secret references of the keys the directory publishes, each proven by its own signature: the key in use, and while a rotation lasts the one before or after it
      --valid-for <VALID_FOR>        How long the directory's signatures stay valid, in milliseconds, after which it is exported again; seven days when none is given
      --json                         Prints the result as JSON
      --attached                     Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon                       Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>                 When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help                         Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store --help
Calls the catalog's `key_value_store` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  get        Answers the JSON value a Run's Key-value store holds for a key, as committed, or null when the key is absent. A key holding bytes fails with `value_invalid`, naming `getBytes`. Takes no writer lock, so it reads a Run no program holds without resuming it. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), it reads what the attempt staged first
  set        Commits a JSON value for a key in a live Run's Key-value store, replacing the one it held: the last commit wins. A null value fails with `value_invalid`; remove a key with `delete`. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), the write is staged with the attempt and commits or is discarded with it; once the attempt ended, it fails with `attempt_ended`
  get-bytes  Answers the bytes and content type a Run's Key-value store holds for a key, as committed, or null when the key is absent. A key holding JSON fails with `value_invalid`, naming `get`. Takes no writer lock. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), it reads what the attempt staged first
  set-bytes  Commits bytes with their content type for a key in a live Run's Key-value store, replacing what it held: the last commit wins. Bytes over 1 MiB are kept as a file in the Run's folder, which its Checkpoints carry
  delete     Removes a key from a live Run's Key-value store and answers whether it existed. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), the removal is staged with the attempt, and whether the key existed counts what the attempt staged
  keys       Sends the committed keys of a Run's Key-value store that start with `prefix`, in the order of their Unicode scalar values, in `$/key` notifications, one per credit granted with `$/credit`, then answers how many it sent. Takes no writer lock

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store get --help
Answers the JSON value a Run's Key-value store holds for a key, as committed, or null when the key is absent. A key holding bytes fails with `value_invalid`, naming `getBytes`. Takes no writer lock, so it reads a Run no program holds without resuming it. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), it reads what the attempt staged first

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store get [OPTIONS] --key <KEY> --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --attempt <ATTEMPT>  The crawl attempt whose handler's `ctx.run` reads or deletes the key: a read sees what the attempt staged, and a delete is staged with the attempt (ADR 0093). Absent, the call reads or commits at once
      --key <KEY>          The key
      --run-id <RUN_ID>    The Run whose store it is
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store set --help
Commits a JSON value for a key in a live Run's Key-value store, replacing the one it held: the last commit wins. A null value fails with `value_invalid`; remove a key with `delete`. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), the write is staged with the attempt and commits or is discarded with it; once the attempt ended, it fails with `attempt_ended`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store set [OPTIONS] --key <KEY> --run-id <RUN_ID> --value <VALUE>

Options:
      --attempt <ATTEMPT>  The crawl attempt whose handler's `ctx.run` sets the key: the write is staged with the attempt and commits with it, or is discarded with it (ADR 0093). Absent, the value is committed at once
      --key <KEY>          The key
      --run-id <RUN_ID>    The Run whose store it is
      --value <VALUE>      The value: any JSON but a top-level null, which `value_invalid` refuses; a nested null is kept
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store get-bytes --help
Answers the bytes and content type a Run's Key-value store holds for a key, as committed, or null when the key is absent. A key holding JSON fails with `value_invalid`, naming `get`. Takes no writer lock. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), it reads what the attempt staged first

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store get-bytes [OPTIONS] --key <KEY> --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --attempt <ATTEMPT>  The crawl attempt whose handler's `ctx.run` reads or deletes the key: a read sees what the attempt staged, and a delete is staged with the attempt (ADR 0093). Absent, the call reads or commits at once
      --key <KEY>          The key
      --run-id <RUN_ID>    The Run whose store it is
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store set-bytes --help
Commits bytes with their content type for a key in a live Run's Key-value store, replacing what it held: the last commit wins. Bytes over 1 MiB are kept as a file in the Run's folder, which its Checkpoints carry

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store set-bytes [OPTIONS] --content <CONTENT> --content-type <CONTENT_TYPE> --key <KEY> --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --attempt <ATTEMPT>            The crawl attempt whose handler's `ctx.run` sets the bytes: the write is staged with the attempt and commits with it, a kept file written only then, or is discarded with it (ADR 0093). Absent, the bytes are committed at once
      --content <CONTENT>            The bytes, in Base64
      --content-type <CONTENT_TYPE>  Their content type, such as `image/png`
      --key <KEY>                    The key
      --run-id <RUN_ID>              The Run whose store it is
      --json                         Prints the result as JSON
      --attached                     Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --color <WHEN>                 When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --daemon                       Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help                         Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store delete --help
Removes a key from a live Run's Key-value store and answers whether it existed. Through a crawl attempt (`attempt`), the removal is staged with the attempt, and whether the key existed counts what the attempt staged

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store delete [OPTIONS] --key <KEY> --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --attempt <ATTEMPT>  The crawl attempt whose handler's `ctx.run` reads or deletes the key: a read sees what the attempt staged, and a delete is staged with the attempt (ADR 0093). Absent, the call reads or commits at once
      --key <KEY>          The key
      --run-id <RUN_ID>    The Run whose store it is
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store keys --help
Sends the committed keys of a Run's Key-value store that start with `prefix`, in the order of their Unicode scalar values, in `$/key` notifications, one per credit granted with `$/credit`, then answers how many it sent. Takes no writer lock

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound key-value-store keys [OPTIONS] --follow --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --follow           Prints what the call sends as it arrives, until it ends
      --prefix <PREFIX>  The prefix of the keys it sends; every key when left out
      --run-id <RUN_ID>  The Run whose store it is
      --json             Prints what the call sends as one JSON object per line
      --attached         Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon           Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>     When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help             Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound sample --help
Calls the catalog's `sample` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound sample [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  echo   Repeats a text back to the caller
  crawl  Visits sample pages, each repeating a text

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound sample echo --help
Repeats a text back to the caller

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound sample echo [OPTIONS] --text <TEXT>

Options:
      --text <TEXT>   The text to repeat
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --attached      Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon        Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

Deprecated. Since 0.1, removed no earlier than 1.0: A sample entry, which the real catalog replaces.

$ goodfetch-foxhound sample crawl --help
Visits sample pages, each repeating a text

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound sample crawl [OPTIONS] --echo <ECHO> --pages <PAGES>

Options:
      --echo <ECHO>    The text each page repeats, a type Collie's catalog defines. No request's params type, which holds `_meta` (ADR 0132), is another type's member
      --pages <PAGES>  How many pages to visit
      --json           Prints the result as JSON
      --attached       Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon         Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound crawl --help
Calls the catalog's `crawl` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound crawl [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  start  Starts a code-free crawl of its seeds, the URLs given and those of `--seeds-file`, whose Records of the Dataset `pages` hold what its flags read from each page, and prints its Run ID

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound crawl start --help
Starts a code-free crawl of its seeds, the URLs given and those of `--seeds-file`, whose Records of the Dataset `pages` hold what its flags read from each page, and prints its Run ID

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound crawl start [OPTIONS] [URL]...

Arguments:
  [URL]...  A seed of the crawl, its URL; repeatable

Options:
      --labels <LABELS>       The crawl's Labels, by name, each with its Payload's JSON Schema or none, and its own settings (ADRs 0082 and 0093): a scope between the Run and its requests. A Payload of a declared schema is checked against it at every enqueue. A seed, a sitemap rule or an enqueue naming a Label the crawl does not declare fails with `label_unknown`, and so does a start under the ID of a Run with pending requests of a Label no longer declared, before any work; a code-free crawl declares its own Label
      --limit <KIND=AMOUNT>   Holds the Run to AMOUNT of the Usage kind KIND: a count, such as `requests=500`, or a price with its currency, such as `paid_fetch=0.50USD`; repeatable, and a start that names the Run again only lowers a Limit
      --project <PROJECT>     The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083)
      --run-id <RUN_ID>       The Run's ID, as `run.start` takes it
      --set <KEY=VALUE>       Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->     Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --color <WHEN>          When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --tags <TAGS>           The Run's opaque tags
      --format <NAME>         A format each page's Record holds, as `page.formats` gives it; repeatable; `markdown` when neither a format nor a field is given [possible values: contacts, html, images, links, markdown, media, metadata, tables, text]
      --field <NAME=LOCATOR>  A named field each page's Record holds: the text of the first element a CSS selector, or an XPath expression after `xpath:`, finds; repeatable
      --fields <FILE|->       Reads named fields, each with its locator and its Value transform, as one JSON object by name in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--field` beats it
      --links <FOLLOW>        Which pages the crawl fetches beyond its seeds: every link its scope admits, its sites' sitemaps, or none; `scope` by default [possible values: scope, sitemap, none]
      --seeds-file <FILE|->   Reads more seeds from FILE, or from standard input for `-`, one per line: a URL, or a seed as JSON
      --follow                Follows the Run's progress until it ends, and exits by how it ended; a first interrupt checkpoints the Run
      --require-verified      Exits 21 when the Run ended with a Verdict other than `verified`
      --json                  Prints the result as JSON
      --attached              Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon                Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help                  Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound dataset --help
Calls the catalog's `dataset` operations

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound dataset [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list   Lists the Datasets a crawl's Run declares, in the order declared, each with its JSON Schema, its key and how many Records the crawl committed into it, a Hook dropped and were rejected into its rejection table, read from the Run's committed state. A Run no crawl wrote declares none
  page   Reads one page of a Dataset's committed Records, paged by key: the Records that meet `filter` (conditions on top-level fields, and the `wall` marker a Record's Provenance carries or not), in the order of `sort` (one top-level field, Records that lack it last, then by number), each value keeping only the fields `columns` names, at most `limit`. Filtering, sorting and column selection run in the engine. A page starts after the Record `after` names, the previous page's `next`, and reads only the Records committed through `through`, the number the first page answered, so Records committed meanwhile never make a page repeat or skip one. A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`; a field name holding `"`, or a `limit` outside 1 to 1000, with `invalid_params`
  query  Runs one read-only SQL statement, `SELECT` or `WITH`, over the runs `runs` selects, at most 100, and answers its columns, its first rows up to `limit` (at most `dataset.query.max_rows`) and how many rows it produced. Each run's tables are table functions that take its Run ID: `records(run)` (each Dataset's committed Records), `rejected_records(run)` and `datasets(run)`, which read its run file read-only; secret fields are never columns. Any other statement, a write, `ATTACH`, `INSTALL`, `LOAD`, `COPY`, a read of a file or URL, or a run not selected fails with `query_invalid`; past `timeout.dataset_query` it fails with `deadline_exceeded`, and past `dataset.query.memory_limit` with `query_memory_exceeded`. A run that does not exist fails with `run_not_found`

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound dataset list --help
Lists the Datasets a crawl's Run declares, in the order declared, each with its JSON Schema, its key and how many Records the crawl committed into it, a Hook dropped and were rejected into its rejection table, read from the Run's committed state. A Run no crawl wrote declares none

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound dataset list [OPTIONS] --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --run-id <RUN_ID>  The crawl's Run
      --json             Prints the result as JSON
      --attached         Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon           Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>     When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help             Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound dataset page --help
Reads one page of a Dataset's committed Records, paged by key: the Records that meet `filter` (conditions on top-level fields, and the `wall` marker a Record's Provenance carries or not), in the order of `sort` (one top-level field, Records that lack it last, then by number), each value keeping only the fields `columns` names, at most `limit`. Filtering, sorting and column selection run in the engine. A page starts after the Record `after` names, the previous page's `next`, and reads only the Records committed through `through`, the number the first page answered, so Records committed meanwhile never make a page repeat or skip one. A Dataset the crawl does not declare fails with `dataset_unknown`; a field name holding `"`, or a `limit` outside 1 to 1000, with `invalid_params`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound dataset page [OPTIONS] --dataset <DATASET> --run-id <RUN_ID>

Options:
      --after <AFTER>      The number of the last Record of the previous page, its `next`: the page starts after that Record in the page's order. Absent, at the first
      --columns <COLUMNS>  The top-level fields each Record's value keeps; absent, every one
      --dataset <DATASET>  The Dataset's name
      --filter <FILTER>    Which Records to take; absent, every one
      --limit <LIMIT>      The most Records the page carries, from 1 to 1000; absent, 100
      --run-id <RUN_ID>    The crawl's Run
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --sort <SORT>        Their order; absent, by number
      --through <THROUGH>  The previous page's `through`, so every page reads the Records committed when the first was read. Absent, the last number committed now
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
      --attached           Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon             Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-foxhound dataset query --help
Runs one read-only SQL statement, `SELECT` or `WITH`, over the runs `runs` selects, at most 100, and answers its columns, its first rows up to `limit` (at most `dataset.query.max_rows`) and how many rows it produced. Each run's tables are table functions that take its Run ID: `records(run)` (each Dataset's committed Records), `rejected_records(run)` and `datasets(run)`, which read its run file read-only; secret fields are never columns. Any other statement, a write, `ATTACH`, `INSTALL`, `LOAD`, `COPY`, a read of a file or URL, or a run not selected fails with `query_invalid`; past `timeout.dataset_query` it fails with `deadline_exceeded`, and past `dataset.query.memory_limit` with `query_memory_exceeded`. A run that does not exist fails with `run_not_found`

Usage: goodfetch-foxhound dataset query [OPTIONS] --runs <RUNS> --sql <SQL>

Options:
      --limit <LIMIT>  The most rows it returns, at most `dataset.query.max_rows`; absent, that setting's value
      --runs <RUNS>    The runs it reads, from 1 to 100: only these are registered
      --sql <SQL>      One `SELECT` or `WITH` statement
      --json           Prints the result as JSON
      --attached       Runs the call on an Attached engine in this process, even when a Daemon runs
      --daemon         Runs the call on the Daemon of the major, starting it if none runs; its refusal ends the command
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help
